WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.310
- [Narrator] Every time
you use your credit card,

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:06.240
a whole series of behind the
scenes steps and fees kicks in

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.329
so you can get-

00:00:07.329 --> 00:00:08.280
(machine beeping)

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.638
But this system has been
getting more and more expensive.

00:00:11.638 --> 00:00:14.610
- Okay, your total will be 14.85, please.

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.100
- [Narrator] This $15 lunch
probably cost the shop

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.820
around 37 to 46 cents in various fees.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:23.820
It adds up and it's why
this place prefers cash.

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:28.620
- We have the cash sign so
that we don't have to pay fees.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:29.790
We save money.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:31.890
We save money on every sale.

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:32.723
- [Narrator] Some businesses

00:00:32.723 --> 00:00:34.980
are even starting to implement surcharges

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.050
adding around 3% to your bill

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:38.820
if you pay by credit card.

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:40.950
And now Congress is getting involved.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:45.240
- Swipe fees just aren't
annoying to the retailers.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.830
They're anti-competitive

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:48.930
- [Narrator] Here's how the
credit card fee system works

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:51.213
and what Congress is trying to change.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:55.140
Your credit card involves
two main companies,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:57.000
your bank, which loans you the money,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.020
and the card network

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:01.500
that handles the transaction
like Visa or MasterCard.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.100
Those two companies handle the
most credit card transactions

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.630
in the U.S.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:07.920
When you use your credit card,

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.710
the transaction runs through
a store's processing system

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:13.140
through the credit card's
network to your card's bank.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:16.080
And there are fees throughout these steps.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.110
The processing systems fee
is generally fairly low,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:22.080
around one 10th of a percent
of the total purchase.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.300
There's a large market the
merchant can choose from,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.070
which can keep this cost down.

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.230
Then there's the credit
card's network fee,

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.180
around a quarter of a percent.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:33.180
And the largest fee of the
system also happens here.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.800
The interchange fee, it's
usually around two to 3%.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.480
It's set by the card networks

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.330
but it's paid to your bank
that issued your credit card.

00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:43.920
- You get rewards for every purchase

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:44.753
- Everything?

00:01:44.753 --> 00:01:48.060
- Essentially, that fee
helps to fund the rewards

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:51.016
that we all get when
we use our credit cards

00:01:51.016 --> 00:01:53.370
- Earn 5% on travel
purchased through Chase

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:54.570
with Chase Freedom Unlimited.

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.040
- Cashback credit cards

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.700
or credit cards with
miles for travel benefits,

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.140
someone's paying for that.

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.200
It can be only a few cents
or maybe a few dollars

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.390
but then, think about
how much that adds up

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.240
when you take into account
all transactions being made.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.290
- [Narrator] Merchants pay banks

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.870
and other credit card
issuers interchange fees

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.280
set by Visa and MasterCard,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.610
roughly 55 billion just last year.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.380
Interchange fees vary by card.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.450
Generally a higher rewards card

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.610
will come with a higher interchange fee

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.610
and businesses have
little control over this.

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.160
They can't choose to not
accept a high rewards card

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.240
because of the fees.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.060
If they want to accept any
of a network's credit cards,

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.000
they have to accept all of
that network's credit cards

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.620
- In the end, what ends up playing out

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.890
is that because the
interchange fees are hefty,

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:44.370
merchants build that in
to the sticker prices

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:48.150
that we all see in many cases,
regardless of how we pay.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.890
So the cash shopper

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.250
and the credit card shopper
are often paying the same price

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.380
but the credit card shopper's
getting the rewards.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:57.330
- [Narrator] If a credit
card network raises fees,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.910
which they did on many
purchases this year,

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:01.710
businesses have few choices.

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.180
It's these rising fees

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.460
that has Congress wanting
the credit card system

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:08.010
to look more like the debit card system.

00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:10.650
When you use your debit card,
the transaction looks similar.

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.090
It runs through the source processor,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:13.800
through a card network, to your bank.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.150
But in 2010,

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:18.840
Congress passed the Durbin
amendment, that same Durbin.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.390
It lowered and set a cap
on the interchange fees

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.970
that card networks
could set for many banks

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.340
and it made banks enable a second network

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.720
that merchants could use,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.010
instead of the card network

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.480
that's on the front of your card.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.540
- So the idea here is merchants
should have the choice

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:35.220
of being able to choose

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.470
between the more
affordable of the networks.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:41.130
So if sending the
transaction over the network

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.340
that's not Visa or MasterCard
is cheaper for the merchant,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.230
then why shouldn't it be able to do that?

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:49.140
So it was essentially
meant to inject competition

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.960
into the debit card
space while also enabling

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:54.840
for lower costs potentially for merchants.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:55.980
- [Narrator] And that's what a Democrat

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.460
and Republican senator want
to do to the credit system.

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.920
Their bill isn't imposing
to cap on the fees,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:03.750
but it would require larger banks

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:05.610
that use Visa or MasterCard networks

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.440
to enable a second network

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.930
to make it competitive
like the debit system,

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.910
which should lower fees.

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.060
Banks and credit card companies
aren't thrilled about it.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.220
- There's a potential ripple effect here.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:21.030
If the banks are receiving
lower interchange fees

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.340
than they currently are,
and interchange fees

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:25.920
are funding credit card rewards programs,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.380
what would happen to
those rewards programs?

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.250
- [Narrator] Banks are concerned

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.140
that this legislation
would limit the amount

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:32.670
of rewards they could offer.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.430
And the networks also argue
their fees go towards things

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.890
like network security and innovation.

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.230
Merchants, especially small businesses

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:43.710
are concerned that the increasing
fees are becoming too much

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.960
on top of already rising prices.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.970
- There are two sides to this argument.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:49.950
Credit cards incentivize people

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.020
to spend more than they otherwise would.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.270
Credit cards result in more sales

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:55.890
than merchants would otherwise get.

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:58.020
And there are also costs associated

00:04:58.020 --> 00:04:59.670
with not accepting credit cards.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.800
Like if you're a cash-only business,

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.770
being concerned about cash
theft and other issues.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.997
On the other side, the
merchant industry is saying,

00:05:06.997 --> 00:05:09.660
"Yeah, but our costs continue to rise

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.820
as credit cards become
more and more common

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:13.710
for consumers to use when they're shopping

00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:15.210
and fees increase."

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:16.740
- [Narrator] Businesses have few options

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.450
to offset these rising fees.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.440
So more and more places are
passing them along to consumers.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.530
So if the price to use credit
cards continues to go up,

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.390
so might your lunch.

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:28.230
- Thank you so much.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:29.400
Enjoy your chicken par.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.638
- Thank you.
- Have a good day.

00:05:31.638 --> 00:05:34.721
(funky techno music)

